feat: ship a workspace patch in chunks instead of one request

`workspace mirror-sync` sent the whole working-tree patch as one request body, so
the route was unusable on any real checkout. The binding cap is not the transport
one: requests are limited twice, and the per-method limit -- LIMIT_DEFAULT, 256KB,
for any method without its own entry, which this one lacks -- rejects long before
SHTTP_MAX_BODY's 4MB. Measured on a real workspace: 14,229,921 bytes offered,
refused at 262,144.

Raising a cap would only move the number that is wrong next: a working tree is
bounded by the tree, not by anything the transport can pick. So the patch now
ships in 128KB chunks and the server reassembles it where it lands. No limit
moves anywhere.

  provider  gains append(), implemented for the shared providers (posix +
            windows). Documented OPTIONAL and left NULL for the container
            provider, whose backend exposes only a whole-file write_file() over a
            NUL-terminated wire -- an append faked there would truncate binary
            patches. The mirror tier writes through the shared provider.
  server    seq 0 truncates, later chunks append, and only `final` commits the
            head: a head stored against a patch still arriving would be applied
            to a fragment on the next reconstruct, far from the request that
            caused it. The response carries `chunked: true`.
  client    splits the patch and verifies that ack before sending anything after
            chunk 0. An older server ignores seq/final and writes every chunk
            whole, so without the check the mirror would hold the LAST chunk and
            look complete. Chunk 0 also carries no patch data, so the worst an
            old server can be left holding is an empty diff -- a defined state (a
            clean checkout at head), never a fragment of one.

Verified end to end against a real aimee-server built from this branch: 4,516,176
bytes shipped in 36 chunks, stored byte-for-byte at that size, and the
reassembled patch passes `git apply --check` against a checkout at the recorded
base. That is a tree the route could not carry at all before, including under the
4MB transport cap.

Also fixes the client reading the wrong refusal envelope. It looked for an
"error" key while the server answers {"status":"error","message":...}, so a clear
"PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE: 1062125 bytes exceeds 262144 limit" fell through and was
reported as the server being too old to chunk -- a plain refusal dressed up as a
version problem. Found because the end-to-end run printed what the client
actually received rather than what it was assumed to receive.

make lint (48/48), refactor_baselines, check_c_repository_lock (workspace pin
refreshed), module-inventory, process contracts, Go runtime bundle and
test_module_supervisor all pass.
